when I first read "Cluh-Luh" I got the distinct impression that "Cluh"
indicated a long "ue" sound, something maybe closer to the German
umlaut than the English "oo".  It was only after someone else posted
the transcription "cluh-luh" that I realized you meant "cluh" to rhyme
with "whuh", not "whoo".

Remember, 1930s writers used different letters for phonemes than we
do today.  Especially New England writers accustomed to dealing with
words like "chthonic" and "fhtagn" on a regular basis. :-)  So it's
not *entirely* inconceivable that Lovecraft would write "Cluh-Luh"
meaning "Clu-Lu".  Maybe.

